@page "/Error"
@using System.Diagnostics
@using DriveTrack.Web.Resources
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Localization
@* AD-14 turns prerendering off globally, so without this the cascading HttpContext is
   always null and RequestId never resolves. Static rendering is also what an error page
   wants: it must not depend on a circuit that may be the thing that failed. *@
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

<PageTitle>@Localizer["ErrorPageTitle"]</PageTitle>

@* The heading row its two siblings carry, and one heading inside it - the scaffold's second
   heading was never a section, only the sentence below the title written large.

   No glyph. AccessDenied and NotFound each carry one because the glyph *is* the status they
   report; there is no drawing of "something failed" in the set - `Warning` means caution, which
   is a different thing to say - and an approximate glyph on a failure page is worse than none.

   `dt-error-heading` rather than `text-danger`: the utility is token-backed, but the token behind
   it is `action-destructive`, the colour the palette reserves for the button that deletes a
   record. The feedback family is what a failure *reports* in, and `danger-text` is its readable
   member. *@
<div class="dt-page-head mb-4">
    <h1 class="dt-type-h1 dt-error-heading">@Localizer["ErrorHeading"]</h1>
</div>

@* A paragraph, like both siblings' explanations. *@
<p>@Localizer["ErrorMessage"]</p>

@if (ShowRequestId)
{
    @* The identifier a caller reads out to support, in the face every other identifier in the
       product wears - plates, licence numbers, ids - because it is a string compared character by
       character. *@
    <p>
        <strong>@Localizer["ErrorRequestId"]</strong> <code class="dt-type-code">@RequestId</code>
    </p>
}

@* The way out. Its siblings both have one and both say so in as many words: a dead end with no
   route back is the failure these pages exist to remove, and this page is the one that still had
   it. `/` rather than LandingRoute - this page renders for a caller whose request already threw,
   and reading a role off the principal is one more thing that can fail here. *@
<a class="btn btn-link" href="/">
    <Icon Name="IconName.Back" />@Localizer["BackToHome"]
</a>

@code{
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private string? RequestId { get; set; }
    private bool ShowRequestId => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(RequestId);

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        RequestId = Activity.Current?.Id ?? HttpContext?.TraceIdentifier;
}
